Nithi Sarathi 
An AI-powered conversational assistant that helps Indian citizens discover government welfare schemes they may be eligible for.
**The Problem**
India has 3,000+ government welfare schemes across central and state levels — for farmers, students, gig workers, women, senior citizens, and more. Most citizens never discover the schemes they qualify for, because:
●	You need to already know a scheme's name to search for it
●	Eligibility rules are written in dense legal/bureaucratic language
●	There's no single place that asks "who are you?" and tells you what applies to you
Manually researching this today takes a citizen 2–3 hours of scattered searching, if they even know where to start. NitiSarathi turns that into a few minutes of natural conversation.
The Solution
NitiSarathi is a chat assistant that:
●	Has a natural, adaptive conversation with the citizen — asking follow-up questions based on what they've already shared (no fixed script/form)
●	Uses Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to pull only the most relevant schemes from a knowledge base of 3,000+ schemes, based on the meaning of what the citizen says
●	Uses Gemma 4 to reason over the retrieved scheme text and explain, in plain language, why the citizen is likely eligible, possibly eligible, or should check further
●	Returns a structured, easy-to-scan eligibility breakdown — not a wall of text
For the project purpose only 12 policies are used. However, this can be sacled to even 3000+ policies.
RAG — why it's needed here
Stuffing all 3,000+ scheme descriptions into every prompt is slow, expensive, and eventually impossible as the token limit is exceeded. Instead:
●	Each scheme is stored as a numerical embedding (its "meaning fingerprint")
●	When a citizen describes their situation, that message is embedded too
●	The system retrieves only the schemes whose meaning is closest to the citizen's message — concept matching, not exact keyword matching
Important nuance: RAG only finds candidates. It does not decide eligibility by itself — Gemma 4 still has to read the retrieved scheme text and reason about whether this specific citizen's situation actually fits. Two AI-dependent steps, not one, both running on the Gemini API.
Flow of the model

Stage	Input Artifact	Processing Component	Output / Purpose
1. Data Ingestion	data/schemes-list.csv	n8n-workflows/NitiSarathi-Ingestion.json	Structured Knowledge Base
2. Reasoning & Logic	prompts/system-prompt.md	n8n-workflows/NitiSarathi-Backend.json	Gemma 4 Query Processing
3. Demonstration	Completed Pipeline	demo/demo-video-link.md	End-to-End Walkthrough

Procedure:
●	Import n8n-workflows/NitiSarathi-Ingestion.json into your own n8n instance and run it once to embed the scheme data from data/schemes-list.csv
●	Import n8n-workflows/NitiSarathi-Backend.json, add your own Google AI Studio API key, and activate the workflow
●	Copy the resulting webhook URL into the Emergent frontend prompt (see prompts/ for the exact prompt used)
●	Open the deployed Emergent URL and start chatting

Benefit of Model:
●	Who it helps most: rural households, gig workers, first-time scheme applicants, and community health workers who currently do this discovery manually for others
●	Time saved: ~2–3 hours of manual research per person → a few minutes of conversation
●	Scale: the same architecture generalizes to any country/domain with a large, fragmented catalog of eligibility-based programs
Future scope:
●	Add regional language support
●	Expand the scheme corpus beyond the initial ~12–20 used for the hackathon demo to the full 3,000+
●	Add a feedback loop so citizens can confirm/deny actual eligibility outcomes, improving future retrieval and reasoning
●	Persist conversation history per citizen (currently session-only)

